Lobium - Windows font isolation and verified-pack transport through DirectWrite.

The installed font set is a high-entropy, permissionless signal. CSS width probes, local(), Local
Font Access, and character fallback must all see one stable persona collection; filtering only the
obvious family lookup leaves the other routes able to reveal host faces.

M152 routes ordinary CSS through FontDataService by default, while single-process/feature-disabled
builds still use DWriteFontProxy. This patch therefore wires every active Windows consumer to the
same process-cached DirectWrite factory, font set, collection, and fallback:

  FontDataServiceImpl                    CSS family matching and character fallback.
  DWriteLocalFontMatcher                 local(PostScript/full-name) matching.
  FontEnumerationDataSourceWin           permission-gated Local Font Access enumeration.
  DWriteFontProxyImpl                    legacy family/local/fallback routes.

With no font policy and no pack, the helper returns the stock system set, collection, and fallback.
With an active policy, it rebuilds the underlying set from only system faces whose family is allowed,
then appends every verified pack face. A configured pack is all-or-nothing: unsafe traversal,
unsupported bytes, or a registration failure is fatal instead of widening back to the host set.

DirectWrite's system fallback ignores a caller-supplied custom collection and can return an excluded
host face. The helper therefore builds an IDWriteFontFallback whose full-Unicode mapping explicitly
names only families in the restricted collection. FontDataService passes that fallback to Skia's
three-argument DirectWrite manager, and the legacy proxy uses the same object directly.

The launcher supplies claimed-family to physical-family substitutions for CSS using the repository's
metric-clone/class plan. These substitutions do not fabricate PostScript/full names or Local Font
Access records: those APIs expose only real metadata from the restricted collection. Runtime gates
report the metric-exact coverage and the class-fallback residual separately.

